    end_line: 618
    content: "---\nname: pwa-integration\ndescription: >\n  Step-by-step instructions for building a new Progressive Web App (PWA) or\n  integrating PWA compliance into a web application (Vite or Next.js) with\n  offline capabilities, service workers, Web App Manifests, icon assets, and\n  in-app install prompts. Use when building a new PWA or adding PWA support,\n  offline capabilities, or installability to a web app.\n---\n\n# Progressive Web App (PWA) Integration Guidelines\n\nThis skill guides you through building a new Progressive Web App (PWA) or\nintegrating PWA capabilities into an existing web application in AI Studio.\nWhether starting a new project from scratch or enhancing an existing app, follow\nthe steps sequentially: select or inspect the framework architecture, configure\nthe Web App Manifest and service worker, set up compliant icons, implement\nin-app install prompts, and verify installability.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 1. Framework Selection & Architecture Strategy\n\nWhen building a new app, select Vite or Next.js App Router based on project\nrequirements. When enhancing an existing app, inspect `package.json` to identify\nthe project\'s build tool and framework architecture:\n\n### A. Vite Projects (`vite` in `dependencies` or `devDependencies`)\n\n*   **Tooling**: Use `vite-plugin-pwa`.\n*   **Benefits**: Zero-configuration service worker generation, automatic Web\n    App Manifest injection, asset precaching, and seamless dev-server support.\n*   **Configuration File**: `vite.config.ts` or `vite.config.js`.\n\n### B. Next.js App Router (Next.js 13.3+ with `app/` directory)\n\n*   **Installability & Manifest**: Use native Next.js App Router metadata with\n    `app/manifest.ts` (or `app/manifest.json`) and `app/layout.tsx`. Native\n    support is zero-dependency and preferred when the primary goal is app\n    installability.\n*   **Advanced Offline / Service Workers**: If the user explicitly asks for\n    offline caching, background sync, or custom service worker routing,\n    integrate `@serwist/next` (modern successor to `next-pwa`).\n\n> [!CAUTION] **NEVER use the legacy `next-pwa` package.** It has been\n> unmaintained since 2022, causes Webpack 5 compilation errors, and is broken\n> under Next.js App Router.\n\n### C. Next.js Pages Router (legacy `pages/` directory)\n\n*   Place `manifest.json` in `public/manifest.json`.\n*   Add `<link rel=\"manifest\" href=\"/manifest.json\" />` in `<Head>` inside\n    `pages/_document.tsx`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 2. Web App Manifest & Browser Installability Standards\n\nTo qualify as an installable PWA across modern browsers (Chromium, iOS Safari,\nEdge, Firefox), the Web App Manifest must include all required fields:\n\n1.  **`id`**: Unique application identifier (e.g. `\'/\'`). Chromium uses `id` to\n    track the identity of the app across start URL updates.\n2.  **`start_url`**: Entry page when the app launches from the home screen\n    (typically `\'/\'`).\n3.  **`scope`**: Navigation scope for the PWA (typically `\'/\'`).\n4.  **`name`**: Full, human-readable application title (e.g., `\"WorkoutTracker\n    Pro\"`).\n5.  **`short_name`**: Condensed title for mobile home screens and app launchers\n    (e.g., `\"Workout\"`).\n    *   **Length Rule**: Keep `short_name` to **\342\211\244 12 characters** to avoid\n        ellipsis truncation on mobile home screens.\n6.  **`description`**: A concise 1\342\200\2232 sentence description of the application.\n7.  **`display`**: Set to `\'standalone\'` (hides browser navigation bar and\n    renders like a native app). Use `\'fullscreen\'` only for immersive canvas\n    games.\n8.  **`theme_color`**: Toolbar / system status bar color matching the app\'s\n    brand theme.\n9.  **`background_color`**: Splash screen background color displayed while the\n    app loads.\n10. **`icons`**:\n    *   **Minimum Dimensions**: Must include at least **192x192** and\n        **512x512** resolutions.\n    *   **Separate Purposes**: Keep `purpose: \"any\"` separate from `purpose:\n        \"maskable\"`.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] **Maskable Icon Rule**: Android crops maskable icons into circles\n> or squircles with a safe zone margin (10\342\200\22315% on all sides). Do **NOT** assign\n> `purpose: \"any maskable\"` to an icon without padding, or its outer edges will\n> be clipped.\n\n> [!WARNING] **iOS Safari Rule**: iOS Safari does **not** support SVG icons for\n> `apple-touch-icon`. You **must** provide a PNG file (recommended: 180x180) for\n> `apple-touch-icon`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 3. Framework Implementation\n\n### Path A: Vite Projects\n\n#### Step 1: Install Dependency\n\n```bash\nnpm install -D vite-plugin-pwa\n```\n\n#### Step 2: Configure `vite.config.ts`\n\nAdd `VitePWA` to the `plugins` array. Ensure `devOptions.enabled: true` is set\nso the service worker is active and testable in local development / AI Studio\npreview:\n\n```typescript\nimport { defineConfig } from \'vite\';\nimport react from \'@vitejs/plugin-react\';\nimport { VitePWA } from \'vite-plugin-pwa\';\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    react(),\n    VitePWA({\n      registerType: \'autoUpdate\',\n      includeAssets: [\'favicon.ico\', \'apple-touch-icon.png\', \'icon.svg\'],\n      manifest: {\n        id: \'/\',\n        name: \'My PWA Application\',\n        short_name: \'MyPWA\',\n        description: \'A modern installable web application built with Vite and React.\',\n        theme_color: \'#ffffff\',\n        background_color: \'#ffffff\',\n        display: \'standalone\',\n        start_url: \'/\',\n        scope: \'/\',\n        icons: [\n          {\n            src: \'/pwa-192x192.png\',\n            sizes: \'192x192\',\n            type: \'image/png\',\n            purpose: \'any\',\n          },\n          {\n            src: \'/pwa-512x512.png\',\n            sizes: \'512x512\',\n            type: \'image/png\',\n            purpose: \'any\',\n          },\n          {\n            src: \'/pwa-maskable-512x512.png\',\n            sizes: \'512x512\',\n            type: \'image/png\',\n            purpose: \'maskable\',\n          },\n        ],\n      },\n      workbox: {\n        globPatterns: [\'**/*.{js,css,html,ico,png,svg,woff,woff2}\'],\n        runtimeCaching: [\n          {\n            urlPattern: /^https:\\/\\/fonts\\.googleapis\\.com\\/.*/i,\n            handler: \'CacheFirst\',\n            options: {\n              cacheName: \'google-fonts-cache\',\n              expiration: {\n                maxEntries: 10,\n                maxAgeSeconds: 60 * 60 * 24 * 365, // 1 year\n              },\n              cacheableResponse: {\n                statuses: [0, 200],\n              },\n            },\n          },\n          {\n            urlPattern: /^https:\\/\\/fonts\\.gstatic\\.com\\/.*/i,\n            handler: \'CacheFirst\',\n            options: {\n              cacheName: \'gstatic-fonts-cache\',\n              expiration: {\n                maxEntries: 10,\n                maxAgeSeconds: 60 * 60 * 24 * 365, // 1 year\n              },\n              cacheableResponse: {\n                statuses: [0, 200],\n              },\n            },\n          },\n        ],\n      },\n      devOptions: {\n        enabled: true, // Enables service worker in development / AI Studio preview\n        type: \'module\',\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n});\n```\n\n#### Step 3: TypeScript Declaration Support\n\nIn `tsconfig.json`, add `\"vite-plugin-pwa/client\"` to `compilerOptions.types` to\nresolve virtual imports (such as `virtual:pwa-register`):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"types\": [\"vite/client\", \"vite-plugin-pwa/client\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOr reference it in `vite-env.d.ts`:\n\n```typescript\n/// <reference types=\"vite/client\" />\n/// <reference types=\"vite-plugin-pwa/client\" />\n```\n\n#### Step 4: HTML Head Tags (`index.html`)\n\nEnsure `<head>` includes the mobile viewport and iOS Safari meta tags:\n\n```html\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n  <meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#ffffff\" />\n  <meta name=\"mobile-web-app-capable\" content=\"yes\" />\n  <meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-capable\" content=\"yes\" />\n  <meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style\" content=\"default\" />\n  <meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-title\" content=\"MyPWA\" />\n  <link rel=\"apple-touch-icon\" href=\"/apple-touch-icon.png\" />\n  <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/svg+xml\" href=\"/icon.svg\" />\n</head>\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Path B: Next.js Projects (App Router)\n\n#### Step 1: Create `app/manifest.ts`\n\nExport a default function returning `MetadataRoute.Manifest`:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { MetadataRoute } from \'next\';\n\nexport default function manifest(): MetadataRoute.Manifest {\n  return {\n    id: \'/\',\n    name: \'My Next.js Application\',\n    short_name: \'NextPWA\',\n    description: \'A modern installable web application built with Next.js.\',\n    start_url: \'/\',\n    scope: \'/\',\n    display: \'standalone\',\n    background_color: \'#ffffff\',\n    theme_color: \'#ffffff\',\n    icons: [\n      {\n        src: \'/pwa-192x192.png\',\n        sizes: \'192x192\',\n        type: \'image/png\',\n        purpose: \'any\',\n      },\n      {\n        src: \'/pwa-512x512.png\',\n        sizes: \'512x512\',\n        type: \'image/png\',\n        purpose: \'any\',\n      },\n      {\n        src: \'/pwa-maskable-512x512.png\',\n        sizes: \'512x512\',\n        type: \'image/png\',\n        purpose: \'maskable\',\n      },\n    ],\n  };\n}\n```\n\n#### Step 2: Configure Layout Metadata (`app/layout.tsx`)\n\nIn Next.js 14+, configure `viewport` and `metadata` as separate exports:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Metadata, Viewport } from \'next\';\n\nexport const viewport: Viewport = {\n  themeColor: \'#ffffff\',\n  width: \'device-width\',\n  initialScale: 1,\n};\n\nexport const metadata: Metadata = {\n  title: \'My Next.js Application\',\n  description: \'A modern installable web application built with Next.js.\',\n  appleWebApp: {\n    capable: true,\n    statusBarStyle: \'default\',\n    title: \'NextPWA\',\n  },\n  icons: {\n    apple: \'/apple-touch-icon.png\',\n  },\n};\n```\n\n#### Step 3: Offline Caching with Serwist (Only When Explicitly Requested)\n\nWhen the user specifically requests advanced offline service worker caching in\nNext.js:\n\n1.  **Install dependencies**:\n\n    ```bash\n    npm install @serwist/next && npm install -D serwist\n    ```\n2.  **Wrap `next.config.mjs`**:\n\n    ```javascript\n    import withSerwistInit from \'@serwist/next\';\n\n    const withSerwist = withSerwistInit({\n      swSrc: \'app/sw.ts\',\n      swDest: \'public/sw.js\',\n      disable: process.env.NODE_ENV === \'development\',\n    });\n\n    export default withSerwist({\n      // standard next.js configuration options\n    });\n    ```\n3.  **Create Service Worker at `app/sw.ts`**:\n\n    ```typescript\n    import { defaultCache } from \'@serwist/next/worker\';\n    import type { PrecacheEntry, SerwistGlobalConfig } from \'serwist\';\n    import { Serwist } from \'serwist\';\n\n    declare global {\n      interface WorkerGlobalScope extends SerwistGlobalConfig {\n        __SW_MANIFEST: (PrecacheEntry | string)[] | undefined;\n      }\n    }\n\n    declare const self: ServiceWorkerGlobalScope;\n\n    const serwist = new Serwist({\n      precacheEntries: self.__SW_MANIFEST,\n      skipWaiting: true,\n      clientsClaim: true,\n      navigationPreload: true,\n      runtimeCaching: defaultCache,\n    });\n\n    serwist.addEventListeners();\n    ```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 4. Icon & Asset Generation Guidelines\n\nInspect the `public/` directory to evaluate existing assets:\n\n### Scenario A: Only an SVG icon exists (`public/icon.svg`)\n\n1.  **Retain the SVG**: Keep `public/icon.svg` referenced in `<link rel=\"icon\"\n    type=\"image/svg+xml\" href=\"/icon.svg\" />` for desktop browser tabs.\n2.  **Provide PNG Fallbacks**: Chromium, Android, and iOS Safari require PNG\n    icons. Provide standard PNG assets or a minimal programmatic script to\n    render the SVG onto a canvas, generating `pwa-192x192.png`,\n    `pwa-512x512.png`, and `apple-touch-icon.png`.\n3.  **Inform the user**: Explain that PNG icons (192px and 512px) are required\n    for home screen installation and complete Lighthouse PWA verification.\n\n### Scenario B: No icons exist in the project\n\n1.  **Create Brand SVG (`public/icon.svg`)**: Design a clean, high-contrast SVG\n    reflecting the app\'s identity.\n2.  **Generate Icon Definitions**: Reference the icon in the manifest and\n    provide instructions or companion PNG placeholders for full iOS and Android\n    home screen compliance.\n\n### Maskable Icon Design Principles\n\n*   Maskable icons are clipped to arbitrary shapes (circle, rounded rectangle,\n    teardrop) on Android.\n*   **Safe Zone**: Keep all essential iconography, logos, and typography inside\n    the central 80% circle (safe zone with 10\342\200\22315% padding around all sides).\n*   **Background**: Use a full-bleed solid or gradient background extending to\n    the outer canvas boundaries.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 5. In-App Install Prompt UI (Mandatory)\n\nWhenever the user requests a PWA or PWA compliance, **always implement and wire\nup the in-app install functionality**. Never leave installation to\nbrowser-default ambient badges or omit the install prompt. If a user asks for a\nPWA, they expect their application to be installable directly from within the\nUI.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] **Always Include In-App Install UI**: Mount the\n> `PWAInstallButton` component (or an equivalent install button/banner) in a\n> prominent, natural location in the application interface (such as the header,\n> navigation bar, sidebar, or settings menu). The component automatically\n> suppresses itself when the application is already running in `standalone`\n> mode, and provides guided installation instructions for iOS Safari users.\n\n### React Install Hook (`usePWAInstall.ts`)\n\n```typescript\nimport { useEffect, useState } from \'react\';\n\ninterface BeforeInstallPromptEvent extends Event {\n  prompt: () => Promise<void>;\n  userChoice: Promise<{ outcome: \'accepted\' | \'dismissed\'; platform: string }>;\n}\n\nexport function usePWAInstall() {\n  const [deferredPrompt, setDeferredPrompt] = useState<BeforeInstallPromptEvent | null>(null);\n  const [isInstalled, setIsInstalled] = useState(false);\n  const [isIOS, setIsIOS] = useState(false);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    // Detect standalone mode (already installed)\n    const isStandalone =\n      window.matchMedia(\'(display-mode: standalone)\').matches ||\n      (window.navigator as unknown as { standalone?: boolean }).standalone === true;\n    setIsInstalled(isStandalone);\n\n    // Detect iOS devices\n    const userAgent = window.navigator.userAgent.toLowerCase();\n    const isIOSDevice = /iphone|ipad|ipod/.test(userAgent);\n    setIsIOS(isIOSDevice);\n\n    const handleBeforeInstallPrompt = (e: Event) => {\n      e.preventDefault();\n      setDeferredPrompt(e as BeforeInstallPromptEvent);\n    };\n\n    const handleAppInstalled = () => {\n      setIsInstalled(true);\n      setDeferredPrompt(null);\n    };\n\n    window.addEventListener(\'beforeinstallprompt\', handleBeforeInstallPrompt);\n    window.addEventListener(\'appinstalled\', handleAppInstalled);\n\n    return () => {\n      window.removeEventListener(\'beforeinstallprompt\', handleBeforeInstallPrompt);\n      window.removeEventListener(\'appinstalled\', handleAppInstalled);\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  const install = async () => {\n    if (!deferredPrompt) return false;\n    await deferredPrompt.prompt();\n    const { outcome } = await deferredPrompt.userChoice;\n    if (outcome === \'accepted\') {\n      setIsInstalled(true);\n      setDeferredPrompt(null);\n      return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n  };\n\n  return {\n    isInstallable: !!deferredPrompt,\n    isInstalled,\n    isIOS,\n    install,\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### In-App Install Button Component\n\n```tsx\nimport React, { useState } from \'react\';\nimport { usePWAInstall } from \'./usePWAInstall\';\n\nexport const PWAInstallButton: React.FC = () => {\n  const { isInstallable, isInstalled, isIOS, install } = usePWAInstall();\n  const [showIOSGuide, setShowIOSGuide] = useState(false);\n\n  // If already running as an installed PWA, hide the button\n  if (isInstalled) {\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  // Chromium / Android / Desktop flow\n  if (isInstallable) {\n    return (\n      <button\n        onClick={install}\n        className=\"flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg bg-blue-600 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-white shadow-sm hover:bg-blue-700 transition\"\n      >\n        <svg className=\"w-4 h-4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\">\n          <path strokeLinecap=\"round\" strokeLinejoin=\"round\" strokeWidth=\"2\" d=\"M4 16v1a3 3 0 003 3h10a3 3 0 003-3v-1m-4-4l-4 4m0 0l-4-4m4 4V4\" />\n        </svg>\n        Install App\n      </button>\n    );\n  }\n\n  // iOS Safari flow (beforeinstallprompt is not supported by WebKit)\n  if (isIOS) {\n    return (\n      <>\n        <button\n          onClick={() => setShowIOSGuide(true)}\n          className=\"flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg border border-gray-300 px-3 py-1.5 text-xs font-medium text-gray-700 hover:bg-gray-50 dark:border-gray-600 dark:text-gray-200 dark:hover:bg-gray-800\"\n        >\n          Install on iOS\n        </button>\n\n        {showIOSGuide && (\n          <div className=\"fixed inset-0 z-50 flex items-center justify-center bg-black/50 p-4\">\n            <div className=\"w-full max-w-sm rounded-xl bg-white p-6 shadow-xl dark:bg-gray-900\">\n              <h3 className=\"text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white\">Install on iPhone / iPad</h3>\n              <p className=\"mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300\">\n                1. Tap the <strong>Share</strong> button in Safari toolbar.<br />\n                2. Scroll down and tap <strong>Add to Home Screen</strong>.\n              </p>\n              <button\n                onClick={() => setShowIOSGuide(false)}\n                className=\"mt-4 w-full rounded-lg bg-gray-100 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-gray-800 hover:bg-gray-200 dark:bg-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200\"\n              >\n                Close\n              </button>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        )}\n      </>\n    );\n  }\n\n  return null;\n};\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 6. Offline State & Connectivity Indicator\n\nAn exceptional PWA informs the user when connectivity changes rather than\nfailing silently on network requests:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useEffect, useState } from \'react\';\n\nexport function useOnlineStatus() {\n  const [isOnline, setIsOnline] = useState(\n    typeof navigator !== \'undefined\' ? navigator.onLine : true\n  );\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const handleOnline = () => setIsOnline(true);\n    const handleOffline = () => setIsOnline(false);\n\n    window.addEventListener(\'online\', handleOnline);\n    window.addEventListener(\'offline\', handleOffline);\n\n    return () => {\n      window.removeEventListener(\'online\', handleOnline);\n      window.removeEventListener(\'offline\', handleOffline);\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  return isOnline;\n}\n```\n\nDisplay a non-intrusive offline banner or toast when `!isOnline`:\n\n```tsx\nexport const OfflineIndicator: React.FC = () => {\n  const isOnline = useOnlineStatus();\n\n  if (isOnline) return null;\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"fixed bottom-4 left-4 z-50 flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg bg-amber-500 px-3 py-1.5 text-xs font-medium text-white shadow-lg\">\n      <span className=\"h-2 w-2 rounded-full bg-white animate-pulse\" />\n      Offline Mode \342\200\224 Cached data is being used.\n    </div>\n  );\n};\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 7. Pre-Flight Verification Checklist\n\nBefore completing your turn, verify against this checklist:\n\n-   [ ] **Manifest Completeness**: Manifest has `id`, `name`, `short_name` (\342\211\244 12\n    chars), `start_url: \'/\'`, `scope: \'/\'`, and `display: \'standalone\'`.\n-   [ ] **Icon Resolutions**: At least **192x192** and **512x512** icons are\n    specified.\n-   [ ] **Maskable Icon Padding**: Unpadded icons are NOT marked `maskable`;\n    maskable icons have 10\342\200\22315% safe-zone margin.\n-   [ ] **iOS Safari Compliance**: HTML `<head>` or layout metadata includes\n    `<link rel=\"apple-touch-icon\" href=\"/apple-touch-icon.png\">` pointing to a\n    PNG (not SVG).\n-   [ ] **No Deprecated Packages**: Confirmed `next-pwa` is NOT used.\n-   [ ] **Vite Dev Mode Active**: In Vite projects, `devOptions: { enabled: true\n    }` is enabled in `vite.config.ts`.\n-   [ ] **TypeScript Declarations**: Added `\"vite-plugin-pwa/client\"` to\n    `compilerOptions.types` in `tsconfig.json` or `vite-env.d.ts`.\n-   [ ] **In-App Install Prompt**: In-app install button (`PWAInstallButton`) is\n    mounted in the application UI (e.g., header, navbar, or settings menu),\n    handles `beforeinstallprompt`, hides when running in `standalone` mode, and\n    provides fallback instructions for iOS Safari.\n-   [ ] **Existing Features Preserved**: Tailwind configurations, existing\n    bundler plugins, and build scripts were preserved without regression.\n"
    num_lines: 619
    num_bytes: 21001
    is_skill_file: true
    skill_metadata {
